If the Facade is the "Front Desk" that organizes chaos, the Proxy is the "Bouncer at a Nightclub."
The Problem: Dangerous or Expensive Access

Imagine you have a Database class that has a dropTable() method.
If you let the Web Controller talk directly to the Database class, a bug or a malicious user could trigger that method
and delete your entire company's data.

Or, imagine a VideoDownloader class. Downloading a 4K video takes 10 seconds and costs server bandwidth. If 1,000 users
request the same video, you don't want to download it from scratch 1,000 times!

The Solution: You never let the client talk to the real object. Instead, you build a Proxy.
The Proxy implements the exact same interface as the real object, so the client has no idea it is talking to a fake.
When the client calls a method, the Proxy intercepts it, checks security permissions (or checks the cache), and only
if everything is perfect, it passes the request to the real object.